# Lane E patch: DependencyUnavailable metric wiring for app/orchestrator/chat_orchestrator.py
#
# Base: 583b39a (app/orchestrator/chat_orchestrator.py unmodified at that commit).
# Apply from the repository root with: git apply docs/conversation-quality/phase2/patches/laneE-dependency-wiring.patch
#
# SCOPE NOTE (2026-09-18): an earlier version of this patch also added a
# post-retrieval `metadata["provider_unavailable"]` check. That design is
# WITHDRAWN. Codex's retrieval work has an accepted, broader contract (R02):
# `RetrievalResult.availability`, a `RetrievalAvailability` enum
# (AVAILABLE/DEGRADED/UNAVAILABLE). Codex's own orchestrator hook, added at
# integration and NOT part of this patch, routes UNAVAILABLE (and DEGRADED
# with no usable final evidence after country-scope reapproval) to
# failure_layer=dependency_unavailable. This patch does not duplicate or
# compete with that routing. See docs/conversation-quality/codex-requests/
# C5-retrieval-outage-masked-as-no-evidence.md.
#
# What this patch does:
#   1. Adds one shared private method, `_dependency_unavailable_response`,
#      that records the DependencyUnavailable metric exactly once
#      (app.metrics.responses.record_dependency_unavailable; component =
#      retrieval|embedding|generation, availability =
#      unavailable|degraded|exception) and returns the localized
#      bedrock_error fallback under failure_layer=dependency_unavailable
#      (with retrieval_availability in the response metadata when known).
#      INTEGRATION NOTE: at integration, Codex's two R02 routing sites
#      should call this SAME method, passing a real `retrieval_availability`
#      value ("unavailable" or "degraded") instead of leaving it unset.
#   2. Calls it from both existing dependency catches (retrieve() ->
#      AwsServiceError/BotoCoreError/ClientError/ConnectionError/
#      TimeoutError/OSError; generate() -> BedrockTimeoutError/
#      BedrockServiceError), replacing the duplicated fallback-building code
#      that was there before.
#   3. Extracts the retrieve() try/except into a second private method,
#      `_retrieve_or_dependency_response`, because
#      AIOrchestrator._handle_scrubbed_chat was already at cyclomatic
#      complexity 16 (over the .flake8 max-complexity=15 limit) before this
#      patch, from Phase 1's two dependency except-branches. After this
#      patch it measures at 15 (flake8 clean). Verified with:
#        <PYTHON> -m flake8 app/orchestrator/chat_orchestrator.py
#      This method does NOT inspect `RetrievalResult.availability` -- that
#      routing is Codex's own hook, added separately at integration.
#
# INTEGRATION NOTE for the coordinator: Codex's concurrent evidence-first-v2
# change wraps the `retriever.retrieve(...)` call in a try/finally that sets
# and resets a rank-list context token, and touches the `retrieval_query = ...`
# line above it. Neither is touched by this patch. That try/finally belongs
# INSIDE the new _retrieve_or_dependency_response method, around the
# `self.retriever.retrieve(...)` call -- it does not need to move back into
# _handle_scrubbed_chat.
#
# Verified to apply cleanly to base 583b39a with:
#   git apply --check docs/conversation-quality/phase2/patches/laneE-dependency-wiring.patch

--- a/app/orchestrator/chat_orchestrator.py
+++ b/app/orchestrator/chat_orchestrator.py
@@ -11,9 +11,10 @@
